# A Linode personal access token. https://cloud.linode.com/profile/tokens
#
# The narrowest token that runs the read-only exercises is one with:
#
#   Domains  Read Only
#
# and nothing else. `verify` also reports the account when the token has Account: Read Only,
# and says so plainly when it does not - a token that only manages DNS is not supposed to be
# able to read a billing address.
#
# The `records` exercise needs Domains: Read/Write, and writes to real DNS. See below.
LINODE_TOKEN=

# The zone the domain and record exercises work with, by name. Nothing here guesses one: an
# exercise that picked "the first domain on the account" would do something different on
# every account it ran against, and on one of them that would be the wrong zone.
#
# For the `records` exercise this MUST be a zone you own and are willing to have a throwaway
# TXT record appear in for a few seconds.
LINODE_DOMAIN=example.com

# Optional. v4beta to reach the endpoints still in beta. It is a URL segment rather than a
# header, so it moves every request the client makes, not just the beta ones.
LINODE_API_VERSION=

# There are two more variables the harness reads, and they are deliberately NOT listed above,
# because this file is meant to be copied to .env and one of them must never live there:
#
#   LINODE_WRITE_RECORDS=yes            the `records` exercise may write to real DNS
#   LINODE_AGENT_MAY_WRITE_RECORDS=1    ...even in an agent session
#
# The first is the ordinary opt-in and may sit in .env if you accept what it does. The second
# unlocks it for an agent, and belongs on the command line for a single run - written into
# .env it stops being a deliberate act, which is the whole of what it is for.
#
# `yes` rather than `1` for the first, so it cannot be set by habit alongside some other
# switch. What it permits: one TXT record created under a name like
# zz-delete-me-linode-api-harness-<timestamp> in LINODE_DOMAIN, read back, updated, and
# deleted in a `finally`. A record in a live zone is served to the whole internet for as long
# as it exists, so "safe" here means "does not break the zone", not "invisible".
